1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,098
This island was painted
for one grieving woman.

2
00:00:03,968 --> 00:00:05,708
A boat glides towards it.

3
00:00:05,728 --> 00:00:08,188
A figure in white stands over a coffin.

4
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:10,053
No one on the island is waiting.

5
00:00:10,793 --> 00:00:13,333
She asked the painter for
a picture to dream on.

6
00:00:13,353 --> 00:00:15,599
And then the whole world
started dreaming on it.

7
00:00:16,339 --> 00:00:19,679
A composer wrote music from a
cheap black-and-white print.

8
00:00:19,699 --> 00:00:23,039
Salvador Dalí painted it again and again.

9
00:00:23,059 --> 00:00:26,051
And Adolf Hitler bought it,
and hung it on his wall.

10
00:00:26,791 --> 00:00:28,851
How did one widow's private picture

11
00:00:28,871 --> 00:00:32,142
become the island a whole
century couldn't stop looking at?

12
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:37,022
Florence, the spring of 1880.

13
00:00:37,042 --> 00:00:42,125
Arnold Böcklin is fifty-two, a Swiss
painter who has made his home in Italy.

14
00:00:42,865 --> 00:00:48,365
He paints gods, sea monsters and nymphs,
and he is famous in Germany for it.

15
00:00:48,385 --> 00:00:52,765
On his easel that spring stands something
different: a dark little island,

16
00:00:52,785 --> 00:00:54,248
cut off from the world.

17
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:58,168
One day, a young widow visits his studio.

18
00:00:58,188 --> 00:01:00,008
Her name is Marie Berna.

19
00:01:00,028 --> 00:01:04,467
She was born in New York, and she
married a German, Georg Berna.

20
00:01:05,207 --> 00:01:08,947
In 1865, Georg died of diphtheria.

21
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:10,358
He was twenty-nine.

22
00:01:11,358 --> 00:01:15,338
Fifteen years later, Marie stands
in front of the unfinished island,

23
00:01:15,358 --> 00:01:17,178
and asks for one of her own.

24
00:01:17,198 --> 00:01:18,938
In memory of her husband.

25
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:20,708
A picture to dream on.

26
00:01:21,758 --> 00:01:23,848
Böcklin paints her a second version.

27
00:01:23,868 --> 00:01:26,408
Many accounts say it was at her request

28
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:29,208
that he added the two things
you can't stop looking at:

29
00:01:29,228 --> 00:01:34,606
a coffin, hung with garlands, and a
figure wrapped in white, standing over it.

30
00:01:35,476 --> 00:01:37,376
In June, he sends it off.

31
00:01:37,396 --> 00:01:39,856
He calls it the Tomb Island.

32
00:01:39,876 --> 00:01:40,976
And he writes to her

33
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:44,816
that she will be able to dream
herself into the world of shadows,

34
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:48,970
until she is afraid to break the
solemn silence with a loud word.

35
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:52,990
Six months later, Marie
Berna married again.

36
00:01:53,010 --> 00:01:55,625
She kept the island for
the rest of her life.

37
00:01:57,525 --> 00:02:00,225
Why was Böcklin the man for this picture?

38
00:02:00,245 --> 00:02:03,362
Look at how he painted
himself, eight years earlier.

39
00:02:04,102 --> 00:02:06,482
Brush in hand, he listens.

40
00:02:06,502 --> 00:02:10,904
And behind him, Death is playing
the fiddle, right next to his ear.

41
00:02:11,904 --> 00:02:13,817
It wasn't just a pose.

42
00:02:13,837 --> 00:02:17,244
Böcklin and his wife, Angela,
had fourteen children.

43
00:02:17,264 --> 00:02:18,580
They lost eight of them.

44
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:24,120
In March 1877, their baby
daughter, Maria Anna,

45
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:26,200
died at seven months old.

46
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:31,361
She was buried in Florence, in the English
Cemetery, close to Böcklin's studio.

47
00:02:32,001 --> 00:02:35,341
A small walled hill of
graves and cypress trees.

48
00:02:35,361 --> 00:02:38,578
Some writers think he had it in
mind when he painted his island.

49
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:42,978
Where the island itself came
from, no one knows for sure.

50
00:02:42,998 --> 00:02:46,258
People have pointed to a
tiny chapel island off Corfu,

51
00:02:46,278 --> 00:02:47,874
though Böcklin never went there.

52
00:02:48,354 --> 00:02:52,334
A likelier model is the castle
rock of Ischia, near Naples,

53
00:02:52,354 --> 00:02:55,214
where he spent the
autumn before Marie came.

54
00:02:55,234 --> 00:02:57,774
But Böcklin never painted a place.

55
00:02:57,794 --> 00:02:59,212
He painted a feeling.

56
00:03:01,112 --> 00:03:06,612
Three years later, a Berlin art dealer
named Fritz Gurlitt ordered a third version.

57
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:07,882
This one.

58
00:03:08,527 --> 00:03:10,507
The rocks turn pale and grey.

59
00:03:10,527 --> 00:03:13,227
The cypresses grow taller and darker.

60
00:03:13,247 --> 00:03:15,020
The light comes from nowhere.

61
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:18,620
And Gurlitt gave it the name we still use.

62
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:20,139
Not Tomb Island.

63
00:03:20,159 --> 00:03:21,554
Die Toteninsel.

64
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,252
The Isle of the Dead.

65
00:03:24,252 --> 00:03:26,952
Now look closely at the rock on the right.

66
00:03:26,972 --> 00:03:31,422
Above one of the tomb doors, two
letters are painted into the stone.

67
00:03:31,442 --> 00:03:32,692
A. B.

68
00:03:33,161 --> 00:03:34,821
Arnold Böcklin.

69
00:03:34,841 --> 00:03:39,433
From this version on, the painter put
his own initials on one of the graves.

70
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:42,353
Gurlitt knew what he had.

71
00:03:42,373 --> 00:03:45,153
He had the painter Max
Klinger make an etching of it,

72
00:03:45,173 --> 00:03:47,153
so it could be printed and sold.

73
00:03:47,173 --> 00:03:51,023
The museum in Berlin says it was this
version that made the picture famous.

74
00:03:51,763 --> 00:03:53,263
The orders kept coming.

75
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:55,393
A fourth version in 1884.

76
00:03:55,443 --> 00:03:59,109
A fifth in 1886, for
the museum in Leipzig.

77
00:03:59,849 --> 00:04:03,989
In January 1901, Böcklin
died near Florence.

78
00:04:04,009 --> 00:04:05,959
He was buried in Florence.

79
00:04:06,089 --> 00:04:09,909
And that same year, his son
Carlo signed one more island,

80
00:04:09,929 --> 00:04:12,151
painted after his father's design.

81
00:04:14,051 --> 00:04:17,551
By then, the Isle of
the Dead was everywhere.

82
00:04:17,571 --> 00:04:20,511
Prints of it hung in
homes all over Germany.

83
00:04:20,531 --> 00:04:25,515
In a novel, Vladimir Nabokov wrote that
you could find one in every Berlin home.

84
00:04:26,385 --> 00:04:30,205
The Swedish playwright August
Strindberg ended a play with it.

85
00:04:30,225 --> 00:04:32,598
In the very last moment
of his Ghost Sonata,

86
00:04:32,618 --> 00:04:34,985
Böcklin's island appears in the distance.

87
00:04:35,985 --> 00:04:40,125
In May 1907, a Russian
composer living in Dresden

88
00:04:40,145 --> 00:04:43,695
came across a black-and-white
reproduction in Paris.

89
00:04:43,745 --> 00:04:45,427
Sergei Rachmaninoff.

90
00:04:46,197 --> 00:04:50,337
Two years later, he conducted the first
performance of his symphonic poem,

91
00:04:50,357 --> 00:04:51,857
Isle of the Dead.

92
00:04:51,877 --> 00:04:57,529
You can hear the oars in it: a slow,
rocking rhythm, like water against a boat.

93
00:04:58,269 --> 00:05:00,809
Then he saw one of the
paintings in colour.

94
00:05:00,829 --> 00:05:02,409
And he was disappointed.

95
00:05:02,429 --> 00:05:05,689
If I had seen the original
first, he supposedly said,

96
00:05:05,709 --> 00:05:07,449
I probably would not have written it.

97
00:05:07,469 --> 00:05:09,181
I like it in black and white.

98
00:05:10,181 --> 00:05:12,801
Composers came back to it again and again.

99
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:16,363
Nearly thirty pieces of music were
written after this one picture.

100
00:05:18,263 --> 00:05:22,243
The next generation of painters
didn't see an old-fashioned picture.

101
00:05:22,263 --> 00:05:23,519
They saw a dream.

102
00:05:24,259 --> 00:05:29,279
Giorgio de Chirico, whose empty squares
and long shadows changed modern art,

103
00:05:29,299 --> 00:05:32,399
wrote that each of
Böcklin's works is a shock.

104
00:05:32,419 --> 00:05:34,159
The Surrealists agreed.

105
00:05:34,179 --> 00:05:36,241
And one of them more than all the others.

106
00:05:36,981 --> 00:05:41,361
In 1932, Salvador Dalí
painted his own version.

107
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:44,321
He gave it a title as long as a sentence:

108
00:05:44,341 --> 00:05:47,681
The True Painting of the Isle
of the Dead by Arnold Böcklin,

109
00:05:47,701 --> 00:05:49,722
at the Hour of the Angelus.

110
00:05:50,462 --> 00:05:52,682
The Angelus is the evening prayer bell,

111
00:05:52,702 --> 00:05:56,282
and the subject of another
painting Dalí couldn't let go of.

112
00:05:56,302 --> 00:05:58,220
That one is a story of its own.

113
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:00,540
It is not a copy.

114
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:03,740
You have to find Böcklin's
island in it yourself.

115
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:07,574
It hangs today in a museum
in Wuppertal, in Germany.

116
00:06:08,394 --> 00:06:11,334
Two years later, Dalí
was still painting it.

117
00:06:11,354 --> 00:06:13,334
The central courtyard of the island.

118
00:06:13,354 --> 00:06:14,934
The western courtyard.

119
00:06:14,954 --> 00:06:17,414
And he named the disease himself.

120
00:06:17,434 --> 00:06:21,698
In the titles of those paintings,
he wrote a word: obsession.

121
00:06:23,598 --> 00:06:27,018
But in Germany, another man
had been looking at Böcklin.

122
00:06:27,038 --> 00:06:29,178
Adolf Hitler loved his paintings.

123
00:06:29,198 --> 00:06:31,235
At one time, he owned eleven of them.

124
00:06:31,975 --> 00:06:38,275
In 1936, the third Isle of the Dead, the
one Gurlitt had ordered, came up for sale.

125
00:06:38,295 --> 00:06:42,515
It had passed through Worms,
Zürich, St. Gallen and Munich.

126
00:06:42,535 --> 00:06:46,949
Now a Munich dealer sold it to the
administration of the Reich Chancellery.

127
00:06:47,949 --> 00:06:52,489
According to the historian Birgit
Schwarz, it first hung at the Berghof,

128
00:06:52,509 --> 00:06:54,969
Hitler's house in the Bavarian Alps.

129
00:06:54,989 --> 00:06:59,411
Then, from 1940, in his new
Reich Chancellery in Berlin.

130
00:07:00,281 --> 00:07:01,621
Think about that.

131
00:07:01,641 --> 00:07:04,021
A picture made for a widow to dream on,

132
00:07:04,041 --> 00:07:07,854
hanging in the house of the man who
would send millions to their deaths.

133
00:07:09,114 --> 00:07:11,254
Then the war came back to Berlin.

134
00:07:11,274 --> 00:07:16,054
The fourth version, hanging in a bank
in the city, burned in a bombing raid.

135
00:07:16,074 --> 00:07:18,923
Only a black-and-white photograph is left.

136
00:07:19,793 --> 00:07:24,851
And in 1945, when the Chancellery
fell, Hitler's island vanished.

137
00:07:26,751 --> 00:07:30,491
For thirty-five years,
nobody knew where it was.

138
00:07:30,511 --> 00:07:33,691
Then, in 1980, it came back.

139
00:07:33,711 --> 00:07:37,552
The National Gallery in Berlin
bought it from a former diplomat.

140
00:07:38,422 --> 00:07:41,122
So why couldn't they stop looking at it?

141
00:07:41,142 --> 00:07:45,114
A widow, a composer, a
surrealist, a dictator.

142
00:07:45,854 --> 00:07:47,434
Look at the painting again.

143
00:07:47,454 --> 00:07:49,674
Nobody tells you who is in the coffin.

144
00:07:49,694 --> 00:07:52,554
Nobody tells you who
the figure in white is.

145
00:07:52,574 --> 00:07:56,244
The island has no name, no place, no time.

146
00:07:57,034 --> 00:08:00,884
Böcklin painted an empty
island, and left the rest to us.

147
00:08:00,904 --> 00:08:03,204
Marie Berna saw her husband.

148
00:08:03,224 --> 00:08:05,358
Rachmaninoff heard a boat.

149
00:08:05,378 --> 00:08:07,124
Dalí saw his obsession.

150
00:08:07,144 --> 00:08:09,723
And Hitler, we can only guess.

151
00:08:10,723 --> 00:08:15,343
Today, Marie's island hangs in New
York, at the Metropolitan Museum.

152
00:08:15,363 --> 00:08:18,623
The first one is in Basel,
Böcklin's home town.

153
00:08:18,643 --> 00:08:20,412
The fifth is still in Leipzig.

154
00:08:21,152 --> 00:08:26,332
Next spring, for Böcklin's two hundredth
birthday, the four islands that survive

155
00:08:26,352 --> 00:08:29,612
will hang side by side in
Berlin for the first time.

156
00:08:29,632 --> 00:08:31,882
Even Böcklin never saw them together.

157
00:08:32,882 --> 00:08:35,342
It was meant to be a picture to dream on.

158
00:08:35,362 --> 00:08:38,325
Everyone who looks at it
brings their own dead.

159
00:08:40,105 --> 00:08:43,394
It hangs in the Alte
Nationalgalerie, in Berlin.
